  "response": {
    "data": {
      "A": "Own the serial frequency-table kernel. Preserve be14740ab7f14780 as the control: smaller-dimension band enumeration, contiguous scans, unsigned multiplicative hashing, exact key comparisons, and zero-cleared uint16_t counters. Test 32-bit stored keys as one isolated cache-footprint optimization while retaining 64-bit arithmetic and answers. Official bounds give |prefix| <= 998001000, |prefix-k| <= 1098001000, and frequencies <= L+1 <= 1000, so these storage widths are safe. Keep capacity at least 2(L+1), non-inserting queries, empty-prefix seeding, and query-before-increment ordering. Do not attribute the observed improvement over the generation-tag variant to clearing alone; several features changed together.",
      "B": "Own prefix preprocessing and key generation. Investigate the cumulative-prefix transformation from 7261731f7867470f against an A-compatible flat-table kernel, rather than spending further effort on unordered_map variants. After oriented preprocessing, prefix each contiguous boundary vector along the scan axis; each cumulative histogram key then becomes ps[end][r]-ps[first][r], removing the per-band running-sum recurrence. Compare direct consumption with a reusable scratch buffer populated by independent, vectorizable differences. Never assert independence for histogram updates. Retain this transformation only when measured gains exceed its preprocessing and extra memory-traffic costs; its existing standalone timing is weak evidence.",
      "C": "Own parallel work distribution around the fast oriented kernel. Use one OpenMP region, completed read-only prefix data, one reusable private flat table per worker, private scratch if needed, and a 64-bit reduction. Compare dynamic scheduling of starting boundaries with chunk one against contiguous balanced ranges of flattened boundary pairs, decoding each worker's first pair only once using the actual team size. The earlier map-based timings do not establish which scheduler wins after allocation costs disappear. Investigate a serial crossover based on general work W=L*S*(S+1)/2, not test identities. Stay within the existing four-core configuration and compiler support; preserve a serial fallback and avoid per-band allocation or team creation.",
      "G": "Preserve be14740ab7f14780 as the measured fallback. First integrate its oriented flat-table kernel with C's scheduling, comparing dynamic and balanced alternatives separately. Next measure A plus B's cumulative-prefix transformation; evaluate the three-way combination if the existing budget permits. Merge semantics rather than overlapping baseline-relative patches, and never reintroduce unordered_map inadvertently. Measure every accepted complete program with the unchanged harness: gains are not additive, and the unchanged A parent must remain eligible to win. Audit both orientations, complete pair coverage, negative values and targets, repeated prefixes, zero-prefix seeding, query-before-increment ordering, private worker state, and 64-bit answers. Keep tests, oracle, compiler, target, permissions, and budgets unchanged.",
      "analysis": "The source solves Problem A, signed submatrix-sum counting; statement Problems B–D are unrelated to the research-role names. Baseline time is 0.249729 s. The strongest candidate, A/be14740ab7f14780, takes 0.047694 s, approximately 5.24x faster. The other A takes 0.079529 s; C's dynamic and balanced variants take 0.095501 s and 0.102052 s. B's variants take 0.244995 s and 0.248131 s, improvements below 2% without enough evidence to establish robustness. These results prioritize compact, allocation-free counting and then measured four-core parallelism. Preserve O(S²L) work with S=min(m,n), L=max(m,n). Negative entries require exact prefix-frequency counting rather than a monotone sliding window. A+C and cumulative-prefix+A integrations remain unmeasured; retain only demonstrated whole-program improvements."
    },
